<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Turns WUH010 and WUH013 on for all five gate projects: WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck (Runtime/),
  EditorCheck (Runtime/ + Editor/), TestCheck (Tests/Runtime/), EditorTestCheck (Tests/Editor/) and
  IntegrationCheck (Runtime/Integrations/). The fifth was added with the project itself (#687) and
  costs nothing: all three of its configurations compile clean with WUH010 on, so the DI tree
  contributes ZERO of the WUH010 sites counted below.

  The test trees were exempt until #653. MEASURED with the four trees of the day wired up: 346
  sites, 286 of them
  under Tests/. 156 of those are in ten fixtures whose SUBJECT is the indexer - the dictionary,
  sorted-dictionary, IntMap and cache suites, where `Assert.AreEqual(1, map["a"])` IS the behaviour
  asserted and TryGetValue would delete it. Those ten carry a file-level
  `#pragma warning disable WUH010` naming the subject, which is a decision a reader meets in the
  file it applies to. The other 130 were the accident the rule is for - a test reading a produced
  map by key and dying with KeyNotFoundException instead of a readable assertion failure - and now
  read through Tests/Core/DictionaryAssertions.ValueFor, which fails naming the key.

  WUH010 (a dictionary read through the key indexer, where TryGetValue reports the miss) is one of the
  two members of the WUH### family that ship off by default, WUH013 being the other: a read of a key that is known present is
  correct and ubiquitous, so on by default it would bury the correctness rules on a consumer's first build
  after a package upgrade. The package still holds itself to it, and this file is how.

  WHY A RULESET AND NOT AN .editorconfig. `dotnet_diagnostic.WUH010.severity` is matched against the
  SOURCE FILE's path. All five projects compile files out of Runtime/, Editor/ and Tests/ - none
  of which live under Generator~ - so a Generator~/.editorconfig
  would not reach a single analyzed file, and a repository-root one would also be read by Unity's
  own compilation of those same trees, which is the thing this must not change. A ruleset is
  attached to the PROJECT, and it covers nothing a consumer builds.

  Generator~/Directory.Build.props makes warnings errors, so a WUH010 finding fails these builds.
  That is the point: it is the package's own opt-in, made binding.

  WUH013 is also enabled for all five projects after #671 completed the remaining loop migration.
  The closing census found 306 sites: Editor 187, Tests 95, Runtime 24 (22 in integrations).
  A persistent audit found 12 more eligible sites in excluded Editor sources, and three SINGLE_THREADED pool sites, completing 321 sites.
  Ordinary array/List reads use foreach; in-place compaction and collection mutation retain their
  indexed traversal. The analyzer recognizes direct mutation, and indirect teardown mutation has
  a narrowly scoped, reasoned suppression. Consumer defaults remain unchanged.
-->
<RuleSet Name="Unity Helpers production check projects" Description="Package-internal analyzer opt-ins for the Generator~ gates over Runtime/, Editor/ and Tests/." ToolsVersion="15.0">
  <Rules AnalyzerId="WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers" RuleNamespace="WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers">
    <Rule Id="WUH010" Action="Warning" />
    <Rule Id="WUH013" Action="Warning" />
  </Rules>
</RuleSet>
